\b Diffusion Models\par
Diffusion models\b0  are the technology behind many modern image generators, including systems similar to DALL\'b7E, Stable Diffusion, and Midjourney.\par
\b Core Idea\par
\b0 A diffusion model learns how to create images by reversing a process of adding noise.\par
\b Advantages\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b0 High image quality\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Excellent realism\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Good prompt understanding\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Stable training\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Can generate very diverse images\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b Disadvantages\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b0 Computationally expensive\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Traditionally slower than GANs\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\par
\b GANs (Generative Adversarial Networks)\par
GANs\b0  were one of the earliest breakthroughs in image generation, introduced by Ian Goodfellow in 2014.\par
\b Core Idea\par
\b0 A GAN consists of two neural networks competing against each other:\par
\b 1. Generator\par
\b0 Creates fake images.\par
\b 2. Discriminator\par
\b0 Determines whether an image is real or fake.\par
The two models play a game:\par
Generator --> Fake Image\par
                  \f2\u8595?\f1\lang16393\par
Discriminator --> Real or Fake?\par
The generator tries to fool the discriminator.\par
The discriminator tries not to get fooled.\par
Over time both improve until the generator produces highly realistic images.\par
\b Simple Analogy\par
\b0 Think of:\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0 A counterfeiter (Generator)\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}A detective (Discriminator)\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0 The counterfeiter keeps making better fake paintings.\par
The detective keeps getting better at spotting fakes.\par
Eventually the fakes become extremely convincing.\par
\b Advantages\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b0 Fast image generation\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Sharp images\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Efficient inference\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b Disadvantages\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b0 Difficult training process\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Training instability\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Mode collapse (generates similar images repeatedly)\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Less diversity than diffusion models\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\par
\par
\b What Are Transformers?\par
Transformers\b0  are a type of neural network architecture introduced in the 2017 research paper \b "Attention Is All You Need."\b0  They revolutionized AI by enabling models to process and understand sequences of data, especially human language, far more effectively than previous approaches.\par
Today, Transformers are the foundation of many modern AI systems, including:\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0 GPT\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}ChatGPT\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Copilot\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Gemini\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Claude\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}DALL\'b7E (parts of it)\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Many translation and speech-recognition models\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\par
\b Why Were Transformers Created?\par
\b0 Before Transformers, AI models primarily used:\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0\b RNNs (Recurrent Neural Networks)\b0\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\b LSTMs (Long Short-Term Memory Networks)\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0 These models processed text one word at a time, making them:\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0 Slow to train\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Difficult to scale\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Poor at remembering very long contexts\par

\pard\widctlpar\sb100\sa100\sl300\slmult0 For example:\par
"The cat that lived in the house near the park chased a mouse because it was hungry."\par
An older model might struggle to determine what "it" refers to.\par
Transformers solved this problem using a mechanism called \b Attention\b0 .\par
\par
\b The Core Idea: Attention\par
\b0 Attention allows the model to look at all words in a sentence simultaneously and determine which words are most important for understanding each other.\par
Consider:\par
"The animal didn't cross the road because it was too tired."\par
To understand "it," the model pays special attention to the word "animal."\par
This process is called \b Self-Attention\b0  because the sentence attends to itself.\par
\par
